# THE routing table. One copy, every deployment.
#
# This file replaced a fork: k8s/caddy/Caddyfile (GKE, 386 lines, TLS) and
# k8s/self-hosted/Caddyfile (AKS, 197 lines, plain HTTP) were the same routing
# table maintained twice, and they had already drifted — the AKS copy silently
# dropped kanninja.com, silicaui.com and admin.wize.works. A platform that forks
# its ingress config per cloud provider is not portable, and the drift is what
# proves it. There is now exactly one copy and no provider knows about it.
#
# WHAT VARIES BETWEEN DEPLOYMENTS, and it is not the cloud:
#
#   Clusters with a public IP (GKE, AKS, EKS — anywhere a
#   `Service type=LoadBalancer` gets an address) serve HTTPS and mint their own
#   certificates via ACME. Nothing here is provider-specific: type=LoadBalancer
#   is implemented identically by all three, and ACME does not know or care
#   which cloud it is running on.
#
#   A workstation cluster has no public IP and no inbound path, so an ACME
#   challenge can never reach it. It serves plain HTTP behind a Cloudflare
#   Tunnel, which terminates TLS at the edge.
#
# That axis — "is there an inbound path" — is a real topology distinction. "Is
# this GKE or AKS" is not, and encoding it as one was the original mistake.
#
# The difference is carried by two environment variables and one 8-line file:
#
#   SX_SCHEME    https (default) | http
#   SX_CATCHALL  :443  (default) | :80
#   mode.caddy   defines the (tls_policy) snippet every site block imports, plus
#                the :80 → :443 redirect that only makes sense with TLS.
#
# `{$VAR:default}` is Caddy's env substitution. `{$SX_CATCHALL::443}` reads as
# {$ + SX_CATCHALL + : + :443 + } — the default just happens to start with a
# colon. Both defaults are the CLOUD values, so a new cluster is correct with no
# env set at all and the laptop is the case that opts out.
{
	email ops@sparx.works
	admin off

	# Honour the forwarded client IP when a proxy sits in front (the tunnel, in
	# the workstation case). With a LoadBalancer + `externalTrafficPolicy: Local`
	# the client IP arrives directly and is public, so this never matches and
	# Caddy uses the peer address — correct in both topologies, which is why it
	# is unconditional.
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static private_ranges
	}

	# On-demand TLS — issues a certificate for a tenant subdomain
	# (<slug>.sparx.zone, at ANY depth) or a custom domain (shop.merchant.com) on
	# its first HTTPS request, validated against the API before issuance so the
	# endpoint cannot be used to mint certificates for arbitrary names.
	#
	# This is what makes multi-site work: an ACME certificate is minted per
	# hostname, so `site.tenant.sparx.zone` gets its own and the one-label limit
	# of a wildcard certificate never applies. Nothing needs a wildcard, a paid
	# certificate product, or a flattened hostname scheme.
	#
	# Ignored in workstation mode, where no site block imports an on-demand
	# policy.
	# NOTE: `interval` and `burst` used to sit here and were REMOVED — current
	# Caddy rejects them outright ("the on_demand_tls 'interval' option is no
	# longer supported") and refuses to start. The deployment pulls the floating
	# `caddy:2-alpine` tag, so the old config was one image refresh away from a
	# total ingress outage on a cluster nobody had touched. Rate limiting now
	# lives behind the ask endpoint, which is the right place for it: only
	# api-rest knows whether a hostname is a real tenant.
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://api-rest.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000/internal/domain-check
	}
}

# Defines (tls_policy) — and, in cloud mode, the :80 → :443 redirect. Kustomize
# mounts the cloud copy from this directory or the workstation copy from
# k8s/local. Imported before first use.
import mode.caddy

# Baseline security headers (docs/50 §4). Conservative on purpose: HSTS
# (no includeSubDomains/preload, to avoid surprising tenant custom domains),
# nosniff, clickjacking protection, and a tight referrer policy. Deliberately
# NO Content-Security-Policy or Permissions-Policy here — those need per-app
# tuning (Stripe/checkout, PostHog, Satori) and land as a Report-Only pass.
(security_headers) {
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
	}
}

# ----- Platform hostnames -----
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://api.sparx.works {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy api-rest.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

# media.sparx.works — public CDN domain for transcoded media variants.
# Cloudflare caches everything for a year; the origin only sees a sliver of
# traffic. The path under it is identical to api.sparx.works so
# MEDIA_PUBLIC_URL is a single env swap.
#
# NOTE: do NOT stamp a blanket `Cache-Control: immutable` here. api-rest owns
# cache semantics per-path — immutable on variant bytes, 86400 on the resolver
# redirect, and a deliberate 60s while an image is still transcoding
# (services/api-rest/src/routes/v1/public/media.ts). A blanket header overrode
# that 60s window AND — worse — cached 404/422 ERROR responses for a year, so a
# variant that wasn't ready at first fetch stayed permanently broken at the
# Cloudflare edge.
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://media.sparx.works {
	import tls_policy
	header {
		# Strip any cookies merchant proxies might forward — the CDN variants
		# are pure content.
		-Set-Cookie
	}
	reverse_proxy api-rest.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

# media-direct.sparx.works — the SAME media routes, on a hostname that BYPASSES
# Cloudflare's proxy. Instagram/Threads/Pinterest publish an image by handing
# THEIR servers an image_url to fetch; Cloudflare answers any `Range: bytes=0-`
# on a cacheable media object with a `206 Partial Content`, and those platforms
# reject a 206 — the image silently drops and the post goes out text-only. The
# ORIGIN returns a clean 200 to a ranged request (Caddy/api-rest ignore Range),
# so a host that skips Cloudflare fixes them. Facebook + LinkedIn sidestep this
# by BYTE-uploading and keep media.sparx.works. The social-worker points
# MEDIA_DIRECT_BASE_URL here for the URL-fetch platforms only.
#
# Grey-cloud (DNS-only) DNS → on-demand cert lands directly on this origin,
# which is possible again now that there is a public IP to point at.
# Allow-listed in api-rest /internal/domain-check PLATFORM_HOSTNAMES.
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://media-direct.sparx.works {
	import tls_policy
	header {
		-Set-Cookie
	}
	reverse_proxy api-rest.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

{$SX_SCHEME:https}://graphql.sparx.works {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy api-graphql.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3200
}

{$SX_SCHEME:https}://mcp.sparx.works {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy api-mcp.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

# Canonical shopper-facing site MCP host (docs/113). A reserved slug, so no
# tenant owns it; authorized as a static platform host in
# /internal/domain-check. The store is named in the path
# (/s/<tenant>[/<property>]/mcp) since a fixed host can't encode it.
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://mcp.sparx.zone {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy mcp-site.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3200
}

# The staff app. Served by the workbench (apps/workbench), which replaced the
# decommissioned dashboard here. app.sparx.works is also sparx's MCP OAuth
# authorization-server origin (`SPARX_APP_URL`, which `<BRAND>_ACCOUNT_URL` falls
# back to because this console mounts Better Auth itself) and the Better Auth
# origin — the workbench serves the /.well-known discovery docs + /oauth/consent.
# Piggles' equivalent is getpiggles.com, not this host.
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://app.sparx.works {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy workbench.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

# Legacy host from when the workbench ran beside the dashboard. It now lives at
# app.sparx.works (the canonical staff URL), so send this subdomain there — a
# permanent redirect keeps any old bookmark/link working. Better Auth cookies +
# the OAuth origin are scoped to app.sparx.works, so this host must NOT serve
# the app itself.
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://workbench.sparx.works {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	redir https://app.sparx.works{uri} permanent
}

{$SX_SCHEME:https}://sparx.works, {$SX_SCHEME:https}://www.sparx.works {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers

	# Social-card images are proxied with response BUFFERING (`flush_interval
	# -1`) so Caddy emits a real Content-Length instead of streaming them back
	# chunked. LinkedIn's image fetcher sizes an image from Content-Length before
	# it will download it; without the header it reports "cannot find an image"
	# even though the URL returns 200 image/png and the og:image tags are
	# complete. Browsers, Slack and X all tolerate the chunked response, so this
	# presented as LinkedIn-only.
	#
	# Deliberately scoped to the two Next metadata-image conventions rather than
	# applied to the whole site: `flush_interval -1` disables streaming, which
	# would break the Server-Sent Events the chat widget uses.
	@social path */opengraph-image */twitter-image
	reverse_proxy @social web.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000 {
		flush_interval -1
	}

	reverse_proxy web.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

# ----- Piggles (the sister brand, same cluster, same namespace) -----
#
# Three registrable domains, and the split between them is load-bearing rather
# than cosmetic (piggles/CLAUDE.md, "The three surfaces"):
#
#   meetpiggles.com  discover and understand      — no auth, no database
#   getpiggles.com   the AUTH AUTHORITY           — sign-up, sign-in, and the
#                                                   money a customer pays us
#   mypiggles.com    operate the business         — no sign-in UI at all
#
# Three registrable domains cannot share a cookie, which is why mypiggles mounts
# no Better Auth handler: getpiggles mints a single-use, origin-bound, 60-second
# exchange token and redirects to mypiggles.com/auth/callback, which swaps it
# server-side for its own cookie on its own domain. Both address ONE session row,
# so logout and revocation stay consistent, and no third-party cookie is involved.
#
# They take ORDINARY Caddy-managed certificates (`tls_policy`), not the on-demand
# policy the tenant hostnames use. On-demand calls api-rest's /internal/domain-check,
# which knows tenant subdomains and custom domains — it has never heard of
# meetpiggles.com and would refuse to authorise it, leaving the host with no
# certificate at all. Same reasoning as the kanNINJA blocks below.
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://meetpiggles.com, {$SX_SCHEME:https}://www.meetpiggles.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy piggles-web.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

{$SX_SCHEME:https}://getpiggles.com, {$SX_SCHEME:https}://www.getpiggles.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy piggles-account.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

{$SX_SCHEME:https}://mypiggles.com, {$SX_SCHEME:https}://www.mypiggles.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy piggles-console.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

# The SAME api-rest the sparx apps use, on a hostname that is not another
# company's. The console hands this origin to the customer's BROWSER (via
# /api/token) and the browser calls it directly with a short-lived bearer token,
# so it is the one platform address a Piggles customer actually sees — in their
# network tab, in their devtools, and in any CORS error they ever hit.
#
# One host block, no extra pod, and no allowlist change: api-rest runs
# `cors: { origin: true, credentials: false }`.
#
# Deliberately NOT the media host. api-rest mints variant URLs server-side from
# the shared MEDIA_PUBLIC_URL, so those still say sparx; giving Piggles its own
# would need a per-brand value threaded through the media path, which is a real
# change rather than a routing line. Flagged in piggles/STATUS.md.
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://api.mypiggles.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy api-rest.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

# mcp.mypiggles.com — the SAME api-mcp, on the brand's own hostname, and the
# most VISIBLE address Piggles owns.
#
# It is not a link a customer may or may not click. The console tells them to
# COPY this and paste it into Claude or ChatGPT by hand, and their assistant then
# shows it back to them on every reconnection. On the shared host they were being
# told, inside the Piggles console, to hand another company's hostname to their AI.
#
# And it is load-bearing, not cosmetic. RFC 9728 discovery happens BEFORE any
# token exists — there is no tenant to read `platform_brand` from — so the HOST is
# the only thing carrying the brand, and api-mcp resolves it from exactly this
# name (oauth-metadata.ts). Sharing mcp.sparx.works meant one answer for both
# brands: every Piggles customer connecting an assistant was sent to
# app.sparx.works to sign in and approve access to their own business, on a sparx
# consent screen, while getpiggles.com — the only place Piggles mounts Better
# Auth — served a consent route nothing ever reached.
#
# One host block, no extra pod. `PIGGLES_MCP_URL` on the api-mcp Deployment is
# what makes this name resolve to the Piggles brand; without it the host would
# fall through to the default and answer as sparx, which looks identical to
# working software. api-mcp fails its own boot on that gap rather than serve it.
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://mcp.mypiggles.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy api-mcp.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

# ----- kanNINJA (co-tenant in the `kanninja` namespace) -----
# kanNINJA's workloads live in their own namespace; Caddy reaches them
# cross-namespace via ClusterIP Services (port 80 → the app's http port).
#
# ON-DEMAND, NOT AN EXPLICIT MANAGED BLOCK — and this was CORRECTED, not chosen.
#
# These four blocks used to import `tls_managed`, reasoning that the on-demand
# ask endpoint (api-rest /internal/domain-check) only knows sparx tenants and
# would REJECT kanninja.com. The first half is true; the conclusion was wrong on
# both counts. The ask endpoint carries a static PLATFORM_HOSTNAMES allow-list
# for exactly this case — and more importantly, an explicit managed block cannot
# work on this ingress at all:
#
#   This Caddy is `replicas: 1` on a `Recreate` rollout (deployment.yaml), so
#   every boot has a ~15-60s window with no registered load balancer backend. An
#   explicit managed block fires ALL of its startup issuance attempts inside that
#   window, so no ACME challenge can reach the origin and a FIRST certificate is
#   never obtained. Cloudflare then answers 525 for that hostname alone, which
#   reads like a routing bug and is a TLS bootstrap failure.
#
# silicaui was blocked by precisely this and moved to on-demand; its block below
# carries the same note. On-demand defers issuance to the first HTTPS request,
# which lands on a warm, LB-registered pod, so the challenge succeeds — and
# renewals happen at request/maintenance time, never at boot.
#
# THE ALLOW-LIST IS THE OTHER HALF OF THIS CHANGE. All four names are listed in
# PLATFORM_HOSTNAMES in
# wizeworks/services/api-rest/src/routes/internal/domain-check.ts. Deleting one
# there silently removes its certificate here.
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://kanninja.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy frontend.kanninja.svc.cluster.local:80
}

{$SX_SCHEME:https}://www.kanninja.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	redir https://kanninja.com{uri} permanent
}

{$SX_SCHEME:https}://api.kanninja.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy backend.kanninja.svc.cluster.local:80
}

{$SX_SCHEME:https}://mcp.kanninja.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy mcp.kanninja.svc.cluster.local:80
}

# ----- SilicaUI marketing + docs site (co-tenant in the `silicaui` namespace) --
# A static Next.js export served by nginx in its own namespace, reached
# cross-namespace via its ClusterIP Service.
#
# Uses the on-demand policy (like the sparx platform hosts, NOT the
# kanNINJA-style managed block). silicaui.com/www are allow-listed as static
# platform hosts in the ask endpoint, so issuance is authorized. On-demand is
# deliberate: this Caddy is a single replica on a Recreate rollout, so every
# boot has a ~15-60s window with no registered LB backend, and an explicit
# managed block fires all its startup issuance attempts inside it — a first
# certificate can never be obtained at boot (exactly what blocked silicaui's).
# On-demand defers issuance to the first HTTPS request, which lands on the warm,
# LB-registered pod, so the ACME challenge succeeds.
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://silicaui.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy site.silicaui.svc.cluster.local:80
}

{$SX_SCHEME:https}://www.silicaui.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	redir https://silicaui.com{uri} permanent
}

# ----- jotDOJO (co-tenant in the `jotacular` namespace) -------------------------
# A THIRD product on this cluster, living in a repository of its own. It shares
# the cluster and the Postgres SERVER and nothing else — its own namespace, its
# own database, its own Key Vault, its own pipeline. The Azure side is
# terraform/envs/azure/jotacular.tf; the workloads are plain YAML in that repo
# under infra/k8s/ (its ADR-026 rules out Helm and Kustomize).
#
# Reached cross-namespace via ClusterIP Services on port 80, the same shape as
# the kanNINJA blocks above. Port 80 on the Service, the app's real port behind
# it — so this routing table never has to know that jotDOJO's web listens on
# 3400, its api on 3401 and its mcp on 3402.
#
# ON-DEMAND (`tls_policy`), NOT the kanNINJA-style `tls_managed`. Both would work
# on paper and the on-demand form is the one that survives a restart: this Caddy
# is a single replica on a Recreate rollout, so every boot has a ~15-60s window
# with no registered LB backend, and an explicit managed block fires all its
# startup issuance attempts inside it — which is precisely what stopped
# silicaui.com ever getting a first certificate. On-demand defers issuance to the
# first HTTPS request, which lands on a warm, LB-registered pod.
#
# The cost of that choice is a dependency: on-demand asks api-rest's
# /internal/domain-check whether the hostname may be issued for, and that
# endpoint resolves against the `domains` table, which has no row for any of
# these and never will. All four hostnames are therefore allow-listed as static
# platform hosts in
# wizeworks/services/api-rest/src/routes/internal/domain-check.ts. WITHOUT THOSE
# ENTRIES THE FAILURE IS TOTAL AND LOOKS UNRELATED: every first HTTPS request
# gets a 403 `unknown_host`, Caddy never issues, and Cloudflare answers 525 for
# the whole product.
# THE APEX IS THE MARKETING SITE AND `app.` IS THE APP, and they are the SAME
# Service — one `web` Deployment that splits on Host in its own middleware
# (jotDOJO's ADR-040). So these two blocks proxy to the identical backend and the
# difference is decided inside the app, not here. Do not "simplify" them into one
# block with a redirect; that is the bug this arrangement exists to avoid.
#
# `app.` IS NOT INTERCHANGEABLE WITH THE APEX. jotDOJO's ADR-010 and ADR-018 both
# settle it: a PWA's install origin is baked into the home-screen icon at install
# time and does NOT follow a redirect, so serving the app at the apex even once
# means every user who installed it must delete and reinstall to move. The split
# has to be right before the first deploy rather than corrected after it.
# THE CUTOVER IS DONE. These five reach the `jotacular` namespace, which holds
# the pods, and `jotdojo` MUST NOT be routed to again — migration 0034 renamed
# the Postgres role `jotdojo_app` to `jotacular_app`, so the pods still sitting
# in the old namespace authenticate as a role that no longer exists. Pointing
# back there now is not a rollback, it is a second outage with a slower symptom:
# the pods answer, and every request that touches the database fails.
#
# VERIFIED 2026-08-24 00:02Z, after the fact rather than on a promise:
#   kubectl get pods -n jotacular  → api, mcp, web, worker all 1/1 Running,
#                                    db-migrate Completed
#   the five names                 → 200 / 200 / 307 / 200 (api health) /
#                                    401 (mcp, auth required)
#
# WHAT THIS COST, because the ordering is the whole lesson. The flip shipped
# BEFORE jotDOJO's deploy, on the agreed understanding it would land in the same
# window. It did not: stage 1 applied ~22:51Z, all five names went 502, and they
# stayed down about an hour until their deploy finally ran at ~23:57Z and healed
# it. A revert was pushed during that hour and had to be cancelled mid-flight
# when their pods appeared — thirty seconds later and it would have taken the
# site down a second time, from the other direction.
#
# The reasoning was right and it still went wrong, because it went out on a PLAN
# that the other side would be ready instead of a CHECK that it was. `kubectl get
# pods -n jotacular` answered "No resources found" three minutes before the flip
# landed. The command was run. The answer was read. It shipped anyway.
#
# So for the next cutover of this shape, the precondition is a fact and not a
# schedule: the pods answer first, then the targets move. And note who should
# hold the undo — this file ships in the release's stage 1, so reverting it is a
# push plus a guard plus a pipeline, 20-25 minutes, while `kubectl rollout undo
# deployment/caddy -n sparx-prod` is seconds. The hour of 502 is the gap between
# those two numbers.
#
# Renaming a namespace is cheap in a way the Azure names are not: nothing durable
# lives in one. The rows are in Postgres and the uploads are in blob storage, and
# both of those keep their names (terraform/envs/azure/jotacular.tf explains why).
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://jotacular.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy web.jotacular.svc.cluster.local:80
}

{$SX_SCHEME:https}://app.jotacular.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy web.jotacular.svc.cluster.local:80
}

# `www` is MATCHED BY THE APP, not redirected here — same Service again, so the
# canonical-host decision stays in one place (the middleware) instead of being
# split between Caddy and the app, which is how the two drift.
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://www.jotacular.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy web.jotacular.svc.cluster.local:80
}

# The REST v1 surface Apple Shortcuts and third parties call. A separate
# hostname rather than a path prefix on the apex because jotDOJO hands this
# origin to code it does not control — a Shortcut on someone's phone — and a
# hostname can be re-pointed later without every installed Shortcut breaking.
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://api.jotacular.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy api.jotacular.svc.cluster.local:80
}

# The MCP server: streamable HTTP transport, OAuth 2.1 resource server.
#
# `MCP_RESOURCE` in jotDOJO's config must equal `https://mcp.jotacular.com/mcp`
# EXACTLY — RFC 8707 binds an access token to that literal string, and it is what
# stops a jotDOJO token being replayed against another MCP server. If the
# hostname here and the advertised resource identifier ever disagree, every token
# validates as intended-for-someone-else and the whole agent surface 401s.
#
# Streaming: no `flush_interval` override is needed. Caddy's reverse_proxy
# disables response buffering automatically for text/event-stream, which is what
# the transport uses.
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://mcp.jotacular.com {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy mcp.jotacular.svc.cluster.local:80
}

# ----- WizeWorks operator console (admin.wize.works, sparx-prod namespace) -----
# The Layer-4 operator console (docs/16 §2.4). Access control is enforced IN
# FRONT by Cloudflare Access, the load-bearing gate until operator MFA ships;
# Caddy just proxies to the admin Service.
#
# The console used to live in its own `wize-admin` namespace, applied out of
# band. That namespace was never recreated on AKS, so this block proxied into
# nothing and the host 502'd BEHIND Cloudflare Access — which made it look up
# from outside, because Access answers the first request itself and never
# reaches the origin. It now ships in sparx-prod like every other app
# (k8s/apps/admin.yaml), so there is no out-of-band namespace left to forget.
#
# TLS is a Cloudflare ORIGIN CA certificate, NOT Let's Encrypt. This host sits
# behind Cloudflare Access, which intercepts the ACME challenge path
# (/.well-known/acme-challenge/*) and returns its login page — so Caddy can
# NEVER obtain an LE certificate while proxied (both tls-alpn-01 and http-01
# fail). The Origin CA certificate is signed by Cloudflare (trusted by its edge
# in Full(strict)), valid 15 years, and never renews. Generated + stored by
# terraform (origin-ca.tf); synced into the caddy-admin-origin secret + mounted
# at /certs/admin by bootstrap.yml. The record stays proxied — no drift.
#
# NOTE: `import tls_admin_origin` makes the whole shared ingress depend on those
# certificate files existing. The bootstrap caddy step syncs the secret and
# FAILS before rolling if it can't — do not remove that guard. The snippet is a
# no-op in workstation mode, where the secret is not mounted.
#
# THAT GUARD DOES NOT EXIST ON AZURE, and its absence caused a total outage on
# 2026-08-01. Nothing provisions caddy-admin-origin here (there is no Azure
# equivalent of origin-ca.tf + the bootstrap sync), so Caddy rolled with this
# `tls` directive pointing at files that were never written. Caddy fails config
# load as a UNIT — one missing certificate took down all ~40 hostnames, not just
# this one. The secret currently holds a SELF-SIGNED stopgap so the ingress can
# boot, and the host serves correctly on it.
#
# It serves because wize.works is on SSL mode `full`, which accepts ANY origin
# certificate. The claim above that the zone is Full(strict) was wrong — it was
# carried over unverified and is contradicted by the live zone setting. That
# distinction matters: under `full` Cloudflare does not authenticate the origin
# at all, so the hop between the edge and this cluster is encrypted but
# unverified. The Origin CA certificate is what makes Full(strict) possible, and
# only then is the self-signed cert actually rejected.
#
# Issuing it needs one permission the platform token does not currently carry:
# Zone → SSL and Certificates → Edit (the legacy account-level "Origin CA Key"
# is deprecated and deliberately NOT used). Without it the create returns 1016
# "User is not authorized". This is HARDENING, not an outage — the host serves
# today. See terraform/envs/azure/origin-ca.tf.
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://admin.wize.works {
	import tls_admin_origin
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy admin.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

# ----- Module marketing sites -----
# Each domain serves its own marketing/landing site (a single Next.js app reads
# the Host header and renders the right module page).
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://sparxcms.com, {$SX_SCHEME:https}://www.sparxcms.com,
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://sparxcrm.com, {$SX_SCHEME:https}://www.sparxcrm.com,
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://sparxemail.com, {$SX_SCHEME:https}://www.sparxemail.com,
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://sparxb2b.com, {$SX_SCHEME:https}://www.sparxb2b.com,
{$SX_SCHEME:https}://sparx.email, {$SX_SCHEME:https}://www.sparx.email {
	import tls_policy
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy web.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

